			{"id":"d-abas-w4","type":"text","x":946,"y":3200,"width":280.5,"height":37.5,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":2,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"frameId":"g-abas","text":"reload the window","originalText":"reload the window","fontSize":30,"fontFamily":5,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","lineHeight":1.25},
			{"id":"d-arquivos-dica","type":"text","x":1690,"y":3330,"width":1320,"height":315.9,"strokeColor":"#343a40","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":2,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"frameId":"g-arquivos","text":"Renaming updates the links that point to the note, showing each one first. A pasted image goes to assets beside the note; dragging a note from the tree into the text creates a link; ⌘-click and ⇧-click select several, which move together; the trash is the .trash folder, as in Obsidian — Open the trash (palette) shows what was deleted, restores it and keeps it for 30 days; Version history (palette, the tab's menu or the clock in the status bar) shows who changed the note and when, and restores a version. A double-click on an .md opens the file alone, with no folder and nothing written beside it — and a .json, .yaml, .toml, .xml or .csv opens the same way, in the diagram; the bar above the tabs leads to the whole folder. Coming from Obsidian? Note 37 says what works the same on the same folder, what Verso adds and what does not exist.","originalText":"Renaming updates the links that point to the note, showing each one first. A pasted image goes to assets beside the note; dragging a note from the tree into the text creates a link; ⌘-click and ⇧-click select several, which move together; the trash is the .trash folder, as in Obsidian — Open the trash (palette) shows what was deleted, restores it and keeps it for 30 days; Version history (palette, the tab's menu or the clock in the status bar) shows who changed the note and when, and restores a version. A double-click on an .md opens the file alone, with no folder and nothing written beside it — and a .json, .yaml, .toml, .xml or .csv opens the same way, in the diagram; the bar above the tabs leads to the whole folder. Coming from Obsidian? Note 37 says what works the same on the same folder, what Verso adds and what does not exist.","fontSize":26,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","lineHeight":1.35,"autoResize":false},
