			},
			{"id":"d-focos-k30-t","type":"text","x":755,"y":7043.5,"width":106,"height":35,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":2,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":0,"opacity":100,"frameId":"g-focos","text":"pane","originalText":"pane","fontSize":28,"fontFamily":5,"textAlign":"center","verticalAlign":"middle","containerId":"d-focos-k30","lineHeight":1.25},
			{"id":"d-focos-w3","type":"text","x":900,"y":7045,"width":495,"height":37.5,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":2,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"frameId":"g-focos","text":"Focuses tab (beside Bookmarks)","originalText":"Focuses tab (beside Bookmarks)","fontSize":30,"fontFamily":5,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","lineHeight":1.25},
			{"id":"d-celular-dica","type":"text","x":1690,"y":7230,"width":1320,"height":210.6,"strokeColor":"#343a40","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":2,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"frameId":"g-celular","text":"In the phone's browser, the bottom bar brings Files, Search, New note, Back, Commands and Panel; a split layout shows one pane at a time. Tapping a folder opens or closes it, and its page opens from the button at the end of the row. Press and release opens the menu, press and drag moves, and the … beside the row does the same without a gesture. In Edit, a tap on a link shows Open and Edit link. With HTTPS, Settings › Offline lets a folder open and be edited without a connection, and what changed goes up when the network returns.","originalText":"In the phone's browser, the bottom bar brings Files, Search, New note, Back, Commands and Panel; a split layout shows one pane at a time. Tapping a folder opens or closes it, and its page opens from the button at the end of the row. Press and release opens the menu, press and drag moves, and the … beside the row does the same without a gesture. In Edit, a tap on a link shows Open and Edit link. With HTTPS, Settings › Offline lets a folder open and be edited without a connection, and what changed goes up when the network returns.","fontSize":26,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","lineHeight":1.35,"autoResize":false},
			{"id":"d-servidor-dica","type":"text","x":3340,"y":7230,"width":1320,"height":561.6,"strokeColor":"#343a40","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":2,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"frameId":"g-servidor","text":"UniVerso, Verso on a server of your own, keeps the folders and opens them in the browser: accounts by invitation, owner, editors and readers, several people in the same note, board or file, live, with each one's pointer and name. In the desktop app, Settings › Servers connects through the browser; Settings › Sync keeps a folder on this computer the same as one on the server — and, online, a note, a board or a text file open in it is edited together, live. Right-click a folder › Share… splits only it with another person, reading or editing; whoever receives it sees it in Shared with me. Right-click a folder › Make this folder a table: invites the players to it alone, each note stays hidden until you release it, and a link to what has not appeared yet arrives with a padlock. To set up your own, note 24 is the step by step: the compose file or the install script, the first admin, optional HTTPS and the backup; note 34 is the route for someone who has never set up a server, on a free Oracle machine, with the installer's menu (sh universo.sh); note 38 picks the road by the machine you have — a NAS (CasaOS, ZimaOS, others with Docker) or a VPS — and covers HTTPS, the public address, backups and the trash's limits. Without a server: Settings › Sync › Sync with… ties the folder to a Nextcloud (WebDAV) or an S3 bucket you already have — note 29 shows the steps and how to limit the key to one folder; the password stays in the system keychain, and the phone still needs a UniVerso.","originalText":"UniVerso, Verso on a server of your own, keeps the folders and opens them in the browser: accounts by invitation, owner, editors and readers, several people in the same note, board or file, live, with each one's pointer and name. In the desktop app, Settings › Servers connects through the browser; Settings › Sync keeps a folder on this computer the same as one on the server — and, online, a note, a board or a text file open in it is edited together, live. Right-click a folder › Share… splits only it with another person, reading or editing; whoever receives it sees it in Shared with me. Right-click a folder › Make this folder a table: invites the players to it alone, each note stays hidden until you release it, and a link to what has not appeared yet arrives with a padlock. To set up your own, note 24 is the step by step: the compose file or the install script, the first admin, optional HTTPS and the backup; note 34 is the route for someone who has never set up a server, on a free Oracle machine, with the installer's menu (sh universo.sh); note 38 picks the road by the machine you have — a NAS (CasaOS, ZimaOS, others with Docker) or a VPS — and covers HTTPS, the public address, backups and the trash's limits. Without a server: Settings › Sync › Sync with… ties the folder to a Nextcloud (WebDAV) or an S3 bucket you already have — note 29 shows the steps and how to limit the key to one folder; the password stays in the system keychain, and the phone still needs a UniVerso.","fontSize":26,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","lineHeight":1.35,"autoResize":false},
			{"id":"d-dados-dica","type":"text","x":40,"y":8530,"width":1320,"height":491.4,"strokeColor":"#343a40","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":2,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"frameId":"g-dados","text":"A JSON, YAML, TOML, XML or CSV file has the Text | Diagram switch: boxes to move, fold and search, without touching the file. A `dice: 1d20+5` code in a note is a die: one click, or Enter, rolls and shows the total and each die beside it — nothing is written to the note. `dice: [[Note#^id]]` draws a row from a table or a list with that ^id; with a die in the first column's header, ranges apply. In a shared note the server rolls, and everyone sees who rolled. Roll dice…, in the palette, rolls without a note. A `> [!secret]` callout is the game master's secret: in a UniVerso, whoever only reads the note gets it without the secret; View as a reader shows how. A colour code — `#1d9e75`, `rgb()`, `hsl()` — gets a small square of its colour, and in the editor a click on it opens the picker. The Table tab, in the right panel, gathers every roll, table roll and oracle question: who made it, when and from which note. In a UniVerso folder it is everyone's table: who is there, messages, images, secret rolls, pinned lines and the log kept on the server. Beside it, the Music tab: every folder of sound files is a list and every note with sound links a playlist; the game master plays, and everyone at the table hears it at the same point, each with their own volume (note 36).","originalText":"A JSON, YAML, TOML, XML or CSV file has the Text | Diagram switch: boxes to move, fold and search, without touching the file. A `dice: 1d20+5` code in a note is a die: one click, or Enter, rolls and shows the total and each die beside it — nothing is written to the note. `dice: [[Note#^id]]` draws a row from a table or a list with that ^id; with a die in the first column's header, ranges apply. In a shared note the server rolls, and everyone sees who rolled. Roll dice…, in the palette, rolls without a note. A `> [!secret]` callout is the game master's secret: in a UniVerso, whoever only reads the note gets it without the secret; View as a reader shows how. A colour code — `#1d9e75`, `rgb()`, `hsl()` — gets a small square of its colour, and in the editor a click on it opens the picker. The Table tab, in the right panel, gathers every roll, table roll and oracle question: who made it, when and from which note. In a UniVerso folder it is everyone's table: who is there, messages, images, secret rolls, pinned lines and the log kept on the server. Beside it, the Music tab: every folder of sound files is a list and every note with sound links a playlist; the game master plays, and everyone at the table hears it at the same point, each with their own volume (note 36).","fontSize":26,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","lineHeight":1.35,"autoResize":false},
			{"id":"d-mundos-rpg-dica","type":"text","x":1690,"y":8530,"width":1320,"height":280.8,"strokeColor":"#343a40","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":2,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"frameId":"g-mundos-rpg","text":"A ```statblock block draws a sheet with the layout you choose with `layout:`; the dice roll on a click, and the counters write the number to the note. The layout editor is in Settings › RPG & Solo Play › Statblock Templates. A > [!oracle] callout answers yes/no questions with odds and sparks, and the answer is written into the callout; asking again accumulates answers. The new project wizard shows the tree before it writes and lets you uncheck what you do not want. In Settings › RPG & Solo Play › Dice and RPG, each tool has its own switch. On the right, a sample Fractal campaign: the world, a statblock, three nested Challenges and a session journal with the oracle.","originalText":"A ```statblock block draws a sheet with the layout you choose with `layout:`; the dice roll on a click, and the counters write the number to the note. The layout editor is in Settings › RPG & Solo Play › Statblock Templates. A > [!oracle] callout answers yes/no questions with odds and sparks, and the answer is written into the callout; asking again accumulates answers. The new project wizard shows the tree before it writes and lets you uncheck what you do not want. In Settings › RPG & Solo Play › Dice and RPG, each tool has its own switch. On the right, a sample Fractal campaign: the world, a statblock, three nested Challenges and a session journal with the oracle.","fontSize":26,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","lineHeight":1.35,"autoResize":false},
			{"id":"d-titulo","type":"text","x":0,"y":-820,"width":660,"height":150,"strokeColor":"#1e1e1e","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":2,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"text":"Verso Tour","originalText":"Verso Tour","fontSize":120,"fontFamily":5,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","lineHeight":1.25},
			{"id":"d-sub","type":"text","x":6,"y":-650,"width":836,"height":50,"strokeColor":"#868e96","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":2,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"text":"a tour in 20 stops: follow the numbers","originalText":"a tour in 20 stops: follow the numbers","fontSize":40,"fontFamily":5,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","lineHeight":1.25},
			{"id":"d-como","type":"text","x":6,"y":-560,"width":1500,"height":113.4,"strokeColor":"#343a40","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":2,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"text":"Two fingers on the trackpad, or the mouse wheel, move the board; pinch or ⌘ + wheel zoom, and ⇧1 shows everything. Double-click a card to read the note right here; one more click edits, and Esc leaves. ↗ opens the note in a tab. The Outline, at the top right of the board, takes you to each stop.","originalText":"Two fingers on the trackpad, or the mouse wheel, move the board; pinch or ⌘ + wheel zoom, and ⇧1 shows everything. Double-click a card to read the note right here; one more click edits, and Esc leaves. ↗ opens the note in a tab. The Outline, at the top right of the board, takes you to each stop.","fontSize":28,"fontFamily":6,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","lineHeight":1.35,"autoResize":false},
			{"id":"d-comece","type":"text","x":180,"y":-330,"width":266.2,"height":55,"strokeColor":"#e03131","backgroundColor":"transparent","fillStyle":"solid","strokeWidth":2,"strokeStyle":"solid","roughness":1,"opacity":100,"text":"start here!","originalText":"start here!","fontSize":44,"fontFamily":5,"textAlign":"left","verticalAlign":"top","lineHeight":1.25},
